drills.explanation:
Keep finished values with their load time, and a second map of loads still running:

```js
const hit = values.get(key);
if (hit && clock.now() - hit.storedAt < ttl) return Promise.resolve(hit.value);
const pending = inflight.get(key);
if (pending) return pending;
const load = Promise.resolve()
  .then(() => fn(key))
  .then((value) => { values.set(key, { value, storedAt: clock.now() }); return value; })
  .finally(() => inflight.delete(key));
inflight.set(key, load);
return load;
```

Only a success reaches `values`; `finally` unregisters the load either way, so a failure is shared by its waiters and then forgotten. Starting `fn` inside `.then` also turns a loader that throws synchronously into a rejection instead of a stuck entry.

The tests catch three plausible-wrong versions: a cache without an in-flight map (50 concurrent misses run 50 loads), caching the promise itself (single-flight, but a rejection is then served for the whole TTL), and a truthy hit check with `<=` (reloads `0` and `null` on every call and serves a value one millisecond too long).

drills.starter:
export function createUserService({ db, cache }) {
  // TODO: cache-aside reads; writes that leave no stale row behind
  return {
    async getUser(id) {
      return db.get(id);
    },
    async updateUser(id, patch) {
      await db.update(id, patch);
    },
  };
}

drills.solution:
export function createUserService({ db, cache }) {
  const finishedWrites = new Map();
  const keyOf = (id) => `user:${id}`;
  const version = (id) => finishedWrites.get(id) ?? 0;
  return {
    async getUser(id) {
      const key = keyOf(id);
      if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
      const before = version(id);
      const row = await db.get(id);
      if (version(id) === before) cache.set(key, row);
      return row;
    },
    async updateUser(id, patch) {
      await db.update(id, patch);
      finishedWrites.set(id, version(id) + 1);
      cache.delete(keyOf(id));
    },
  };
}

