drills.explanation:
Normalise the value recursively, then let JSON do the quoting:

```js
const normalize = (value) => {
  if (Array.isArray(value)) return value.map(normalize);
  if (value !== null && typeof value === 'object') {
    const out = {};
    for (const k of Object.keys(value).sort()) if (value[k] !== undefined) out[k] = normalize(value[k]);
    return out;
  }
  return value;
};
return JSON.stringify([tenant, user, normalize(params)]);
```

JSON quotes and escapes every string, so no part can contain a "separator" that forges another part.

The tests catch two plausible-wrong versions. Sorting with `JSON.stringify(params, Object.keys(params).sort())` passes the flat tests, but an array replacer is an allow-list applied at EVERY depth: `{ filter: { status: 'open' } }` serialises as `{"filter":{}}`, so every filter shares one key, and one user's filtered results are served for another filter. Joining with `:` and `&` lets `a:b` + `c` collide with `a` + `b:c`, and a value such as `a&z=1` forge an extra param.
